==English==

===Etymology===
As if {{m|en|smack|smacked|hit}} in the {{m|en|gob||mouth (Irish / Scottish gaelic)}}.

Attested since 1959, from Northern English dialect, particularly Liverpool, popularized via television.<ref>[http://www.worldwidewords.org/qa/qa-gob1.htm World Wide Words: Gobsmacked]</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɡɒb.smækt/|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|British|Australian|slang}} [[flabbergasted|Flabbergasted]], [[astounded]], [[speechless]], [[overawed]].
#* '''1989''' Aug. 7, Glenn Frankel, "Salman Rushdie's Life on the Run," ''Los Angeles Times'':
#*: We were as appalled and stunned and confused and '''gobsmacked''' (punched on the mouth) as anyone else.
#* '''2008''' June 16, Caroline Mallan, "Linwood Barclay novel wins a plug on key UK book list," ''Toronto Star'' (Canada), p. A2:
#*: "I guess the word would be '''gobsmacked'''," Barclay said, of his reaction. "I am stunned."

